module

public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.DifferentialGraded.RightModule.DerivedHomReflector
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.DifferentialGraded.RightModule.DerivedHomReflectorUnit
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.DifferentialGraded.RightModule.YonedaVerdier

/-!
# Steps 1 and 2 of Tag 09LK assembled: the exact `R̃ : D(A) ⥤ D(E)` and its unit

Let `C` be a differential graded category over a commutative ring `R` whose objects and whose
morphism modules live in one universe, let `K` be an object of `C` and let `G` be a family of
objects of `C` indexed by that universe — in the intended reading the acyclic complexes. Let
`N` be the localizing subcategory of `K(Mod^dg(C))` generated by the representables
`h_{G i}`.

Step 1 of [Stacks, Tag 09LK]
(`DGCategory.RightModule.exists_isTriangulated_reflector_yonedaObjK_of_dgCategory`) gives an
exact reflector `L` with a unit `η : 𝟭 ⟶ L` whose values are `N`-local. The reflector
therefore annihilates `N` itself, by
`CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.isZero_of_bijective_precomp_unit`. If the source functor `Φ`
carries every object of the subcategory `Z` being inverted into `N` — this is the statement
that `N` is generated by the `h_B` for the acyclic `B` — then `Φ ⋙ L` kills `Z`, the
descent of `DGCategory.derivedReflectorDescent` exists, and

`R̃ = ev_K ∘ J : D(A) ⥤ D(E)`

is an exact functor with a natural transformation
`Φ ⋙ ev_K ⋙ Q_E ⟶ q ⋙ R̃`. That is what this file records: the hypothesis `hΦ` of
`DGCategory.derivedHomReflector` is not an extra assumption on top of step 1, it is a
consequence of step 1 together with the generation hypothesis.

## The hypothesis that remains

The exactness of `Φ` remains a hypothesis. For `Φ` the differential graded Yoneda functor
`X ↦ h_X` on `K(A)` it would follow from a cone situation on `C` and the compatibility of the
Yoneda functor with cones; neither is in this development, so `Φ` is taken abstract and exact.
Also inherited from step 1 is the universe condition `C : DGCategory.{v, v} R` with the
generating family indexed by `Type v`, which is `u ≤ v` — automatic for a differential graded
algebra. The `E` side imposes nothing: `DGCategory.endDGCategory C K` is one-object.

## The clause that step 1 also proves

Step 1 has a third conclusion: the fibre of `η.app X` lies in `N`, which is what makes `η.app X`
a morphism inverted by the Verdier quotient at `N`. The construction of `R̃` does not use it, so
the proof of `DGCategory.exists_isTriangulated_derivedHomReflector` discards it.
`DGCategory.exists_isTriangulated_derivedHomReflector_trW` is the same statement with that
conclusion kept, in the form `N.trW (η.app X)` supplied by
`DGCategory.RightModule.exists_isTriangulated_reflector_yonedaObjK_trW_of_dgCategory`; it is
what identifies `Hom(-, (L X)⟦n⟧)` with a Hom group of the quotient.

## Main results

* `DGCategory.exists_isTriangulated_derivedHomReflector`: the exact `R̃ : D(A) ⥤ D(E)` with its
  unit and with the identification of `q ⋙ R̃`.
* `DGCategory.exists_isTriangulated_derivedHomReflector_trW`: the same, together with the
  inversion of the unit `η` of the reflector in `K(Mod^dg(C))/N`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 09LK](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09LK)
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace DGCategory

universe v₂ u₂ v₁ u₁ v r

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated
open CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty

variable {R : Type r} [CommRing R] (C : DGCategory.{v, v} R) (K : C.Obj)
  {ι : Type v} (G : ι → C.Obj)
  {S : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} S] [HasZeroObject S] [HasShift S ℤ] [Preadditive S]
  [∀ n : ℤ, (shiftFunctor S n).Additive] [Pretriangulated S] [IsTriangulated S]
  (Φ : S ⥤ (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K) [Φ.CommShift ℤ] [Φ.IsTriangulated]
  (Z : ObjectProperty S) [Z.IsTriangulated]
  {DA : Type u₂} [Category.{v₂} DA] [HasZeroObject DA] [HasShift DA ℤ] [Preadditive DA]
  [∀ n : ℤ, (shiftFunctor DA n).Additive] [Pretriangulated DA]
  (q : S ⥤ DA) [q.IsLocalization Z.trW] [q.CommShift ℤ] [q.IsTriangulated]

/-- **Steps 1 and 2 of [Stacks, Tag 09LK].** Let `S` be a triangulated category, `Z` a
triangulated subcategory of it and `q : S ⥤ D(A)` a localization of `S` at the morphisms whose
cone lies in `Z`; let `Φ : S ⥤ K(Mod^dg(C))` be exact and carry every object of `Z` into the
localizing subcategory `N` generated by the representables `h_{G i}`. Then there are an exact
reflector `L` with unit `η`, an exact functor

`R̃ : D(A) ⥤ D(E)`

with values in the derived category of right differential graded modules over the
endomorphism complex `E = C(K, K)`, and a natural transformation
`Φ ⋙ ev_K ⋙ Q_E ⟶ q ⋙ R̃`; and the restriction of `R̃` along `q` is the reflected evaluation
`X ↦ ev_K (L (Φ X))`, read in `D(E)`.

In the intended reading `S = K(A)`, `Φ` is the differential graded Yoneda functor `X ↦ h_X`,
`Z` is the acyclic complexes and `G` is a family of acyclic objects, so that the hypothesis
`hZ` is the definition of `N`. -/
theorem exists_isTriangulated_derivedHomReflector
    (hZ : ∀ X, Z X → RightModule.yonedaLocalizing C G (Φ.obj X)) :
    ∃ (L : (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K ⥤ (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K)
      (_ : L.CommShift ℤ) (_ : L.IsTriangulated) (_ : 𝟭 _ ⟶ L)
      (Rt : DA ⥤ RightModule.derivedCategory (endDGCategory C K))
      (_ : Rt.CommShift ℤ) (_ : Rt.IsTriangulated)
      (_ : Φ ⋙ evaluationOnK.{v} C K ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K) ⟶ q ⋙ Rt),
      Nonempty (q ⋙ Rt ≅ Φ ⋙ L ⋙ evaluationOnK.{v} C K ⋙
        RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K)) := by
  obtain ⟨L, cs, tri, η, h₁, h₂, -⟩ :=
    RightModule.exists_isTriangulated_reflector_yonedaObjK_of_dgCategory C G
  have hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X) := fun X hX =>
    isZero_of_bijective_precomp_unit _ (h₁ (Φ.obj X))
      (h₂ _ (h₁ (Φ.obj X)) (Φ.obj X)).1 (hZ X hX)
  exact ⟨L, cs, tri, η, derivedHomReflector C K L Φ Z q hΦ, inferInstance, inferInstance,
    derivedHomReflectorUnit C K L Φ Z q η hΦ,
    ⟨derivedHomReflectorCompIso C K L Φ Z q hΦ⟩⟩

/-- **Steps 1 and 2 of [Stacks, Tag 09LK], with the unit of the reflector exhibited as a
`trW`-morphism.** The data and the conclusions of
`DGCategory.exists_isTriangulated_derivedHomReflector`, together with the third conclusion of
step 1 that that theorem's proof discards: every `η.app X` is inverted by the Verdier quotient
of `K(Mod^dg(C))` at the localizing subcategory `N` generated by the representables `h_{G i}`.

Keeping it is what lets `Hom(A, (L X)⟦n⟧)` be identified with a Hom group of
`K(Mod^dg(C))/N`, by `DGCategory.RightModule.exists_reflector_homEquiv_yonedaObjK`. -/
theorem exists_isTriangulated_derivedHomReflector_trW
    (hZ : ∀ X, Z X → RightModule.yonedaLocalizing C G (Φ.obj X)) :
    ∃ (L : (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K ⥤ (rightModuleCategory.{v} C).K)
      (_ : L.CommShift ℤ) (_ : L.IsTriangulated) (η : 𝟭 _ ⟶ L)
      (Rt : DA ⥤ RightModule.derivedCategory (endDGCategory C K))
      (_ : Rt.CommShift ℤ) (_ : Rt.IsTriangulated)
      (_ : Φ ⋙ evaluationOnK.{v} C K ⋙ RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K) ⟶ q ⋙ Rt),
      Nonempty (q ⋙ Rt ≅ Φ ⋙ L ⋙ evaluationOnK.{v} C K ⋙
        RightModule.derivedQ (endDGCategory C K)) ∧
      (∀ X, (RightModule.yonedaLocalizing C G).trW (η.app X)) := by
  obtain ⟨L, cs, tri, η, h₁, h₂, h₃⟩ :=
    RightModule.exists_isTriangulated_reflector_yonedaObjK_trW_of_dgCategory C G
  have hΦ : ∀ X, Z X → IsZero ((Φ ⋙ L).obj X) := fun X hX =>
    isZero_of_bijective_precomp_unit _ (h₁ (Φ.obj X))
      (h₂ _ (h₁ (Φ.obj X)) (Φ.obj X)).1 (hZ X hX)
  exact ⟨L, cs, tri, η, derivedHomReflector C K L Φ Z q hΦ, inferInstance, inferInstance,
    derivedHomReflectorUnit C K L Φ Z q η hΦ,
    ⟨derivedHomReflectorCompIso C K L Φ Z q hΦ⟩, h₃⟩

end DGCategory

end
